where GCPs or Google
Cloud has presence,

00:11:48.770 --> 00:11:51.350
externally as well as--

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:55.100
the number indicates how many
availability zones there are,

00:11:55.100 --> 00:11:58.040
and how they're all connected
via Google Fiber, essentially.

00:11:58.040 --> 00:12:00.440
So to Andrew's
point, when customers

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:02.570
ask me like, well, what
will the latency be

00:12:02.570 --> 00:12:05.160
like for this globally
distributed massive thing,

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:07.040
I'm like, not very much.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:08.600
They're like, how
do you go over RAN?

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:11.130
And we're like, we don't have
to go over public internet

00:12:11.130 --> 00:12:12.200
for the most part.

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:13.730
So that's super awesome.

00:12:13.730 --> 00:12:16.280
I wonder if you want to
touch upon Colossus before we

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:16.730
go to the next thing.

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:17.522
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah.

00:12:17.522 --> 00:12:20.300
So I think the other area
we've always really depended on

00:12:20.300 --> 00:12:22.460
is, we do have a very
large scale distributed

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:26.420
file system that sits as the
basis of all of our distributed

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:28.430
storage systems.

00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:30.512
We published a
paper on GFS, which

00:12:30.512 --> 00:12:31.970
was the very first
version of that,

00:12:31.970 --> 00:12:35.090
I think probably in 2002, 2003.

00:12:35.090 --> 00:12:38.960
The one we use now today is
actually called Colossus.

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:43.640
And it basically occupies
many, many megawatts

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:46.220
of power of footprint of space.

00:12:46.220 --> 00:12:49.110
And we really depend on that
for all of our write operations.

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:51.900
And so that means the durability
of the data, for example,

00:12:51.900 --> 00:12:53.730
is something that we
can delegate down.

00:12:53.730 --> 00:12:55.730
It also means that we're
in an environment where

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:59.030
we can disaggregate
compute from storage.

00:12:59.030 --> 00:13:01.010
It gives us the
ability to respond

00:13:01.010 --> 00:13:06.170
to hotspots on one server by
actually indirectly picking

00:13:06.170 --> 00:13:08.630
that piece of data up and
moving it to another server

00:13:08.630 --> 00:13:11.840
while leaving the
storage beneath.

00:13:11.840 --> 00:13:14.840
Fun little fact-- Colossus
is actually built recursively

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:16.790
on top of Bigtable.

00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:19.610
So this is a place where we're
reusing our infrastructure

00:13:19.610 --> 00:13:21.400
in order to be able
to scale it out.

00:13:23.682 --> 00:13:25.640
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: The
amount of virtualization

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:27.640
that's happening in our
infrastructure stack

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:28.610
is pretty astounding.

00:13:28.610 --> 00:13:31.408
Because again, customers ask me,
what happens when a disk fails?

00:13:31.408 --> 00:13:33.575
And I'm like, I haven't
thought about a disk failure

00:13:33.575 --> 00:13:35.930
in I don't know how many years.

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:37.580
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah.

00:13:37.580 --> 00:13:39.428
When I spoke earlier
to now getting

00:13:39.428 --> 00:13:41.720
a lot of visibility from
everything from the disk layer

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:47.385
on up, Google actually builds
its own disk appliances.

00:13:47.385 --> 00:13:49.010
And we spend an
enormous amount of time

00:13:49.010 --> 00:13:51.552
thinking about the availability
of a disk appliance, the cost

00:13:51.552 --> 00:13:53.780
of a disk appliance,
how fast we can execute

00:13:53.780 --> 00:13:56.090
RPCs against these things.

00:13:56.090 --> 00:13:59.360
We continually improve our
efficiency and effectiveness

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:03.340
of those disk appliances, and
we deploy them at massive scale.

00:14:03.340 --> 00:14:07.550
And being able to have that
incoming pipe of people working

00:14:07.550 --> 00:14:10.280
on, even just the
building of raw components

00:14:10.280 --> 00:14:12.470
that you're building
these systems on,

00:14:12.470 --> 00:14:14.190
it's an absolute luxury.

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:16.857
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yeah, I think
we're a hardware company too as

00:14:16.857 --> 00:14:20.000
much as a data center business.

00:14:20.000 --> 00:14:25.010
So this is the other part
that we basically win on,

00:14:25.010 --> 00:14:26.300
which is TrueTime.

00:14:26.300 --> 00:14:28.070
So walk us through this.

00:14:28.070 --> 00:14:31.160
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah, so I can't
be more excited to show you

00:14:31.160 --> 00:14:34.310
this most boring rack.

00:14:34.310 --> 00:14:37.288
I think one of the most
interesting comments I usually

00:14:37.288 --> 00:14:39.830
get when people read the Spanner
paper is, they're like, wow,

00:14:39.830 --> 00:14:41.263
atomic clocks.

00:14:41.263 --> 00:14:43.430
And then you're like, yes,
you can get them on eBay.

00:14:43.430 --> 00:14:45.950
They're not as expensive
as you think they are.

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:48.120
This is actually our
latest generation,

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:51.560
so this is rolling out
into the fleet now.

00:14:51.560 --> 00:14:54.770
The TrueTime team is
a really small group.

00:14:54.770 --> 00:14:57.770
This basically shows a
fairly standard configuration

00:14:57.770 --> 00:15:00.680
that we have within
a data center.

00:15:00.680 --> 00:15:03.300
There are four time
servers in here.

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:06.790
Two are connected out to the
GSP antennas that you see.

00:15:06.790 --> 00:15:10.023
This provides a
GPS-calibrated signal.

00:15:10.023 --> 00:15:11.440
Two of the other
remaining servers

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:13.995
are actually connected to
two atomic oscillators.

00:15:13.995 --> 00:15:16.120
There are two different
brands of oscillators here,

00:15:16.120 --> 00:15:18.660
so we get different failure
behaviors, actually,

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:21.590
and the same with the
GPS cards, as well.

00:15:21.590 --> 00:15:24.000
And this is connected
into our fabrics.

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:28.210
And what this does is it
provides a really accurate time

00:15:28.210 --> 00:15:30.140
signal with bounds.

00:15:30.140 --> 00:15:32.410
And this is sort of the
principle upon which Spanner

00:15:32.410 --> 00:15:36.250
is built, this idea of not
just saying a point in time,

00:15:36.250 --> 00:15:37.430
but an interval in time.

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:40.240
And what can we do with a
very highly reliable, accurate

00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:41.770
interval of time?

00:15:41.770 --> 00:15:43.210
And that's what
TrueTime provides.

00:15:43.210 --> 00:15:46.960
And I can't speak exactly
to where we are now,

00:15:46.960 --> 00:15:52.590
but it's clearly less than
a millisecond of drift.

00:15:52.590 --> 00:15:54.940
And there you go.

00:15:54.940 --> 00:15:56.530
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yeah.

00:15:56.530 --> 00:15:59.470
This is one of the
cornerstones that

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:01.990
allows us to be the
differentiated technology

00:16:01.990 --> 00:16:03.440
in terms of external
consistency.

00:16:03.440 --> 00:16:05.680
And we'll get into
that, but I think

00:16:05.680 --> 00:16:07.750
it's important to
understand that it's not

00:16:07.750 --> 00:16:09.910
just about atomic
clocks and GPS,

00:16:09.910 --> 00:16:12.550
but about the hard
work that goes

00:16:12.550 --> 00:16:17.500
into building these systems and
the infrastructure around that.

00:16:17.500 --> 00:16:19.150
OK.

00:16:19.150 --> 00:16:23.980
So let's quickly touch upon,
given those building blocks,

00:16:23.980 --> 00:16:27.820
how does Spanner
do a serve request?

00:16:27.820 --> 00:16:30.985
What is the life of
a read and a write?

00:16:30.985 --> 00:16:32.860
I am very excited to
say that Andrew actually

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:34.870
drew this by hand.

00:16:34.870 --> 00:16:35.920
He's good with crayons.

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:36.795
ANDREW FIKES: Woohoo!

00:16:36.795 --> 00:16:38.360
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:38.523 --> 00:16:39.940
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA:
And his writing

00:16:39.940 --> 00:16:41.470
is remarkably good, right?

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:44.773
ANDREW FIKES: I went to
an engineering school.

00:16:44.773 --> 00:16:46.690
So yeah, my kids actually
helped me with this.

00:16:46.690 --> 00:16:47.232
It was great.

00:16:47.232 --> 00:16:48.760
We did it this weekend.

00:16:48.760 --> 00:16:51.340
It turns out that even
an eight-year-old can

00:16:51.340 --> 00:16:54.640
design a distributed system.

00:16:54.640 --> 00:16:58.580
Hers didn't look quite as good
as mine, but we will take it.

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:00.540
So we do get a lot
of questions about,

00:17:00.540 --> 00:17:04.030
how does Spanner work,
what exactly happens

00:17:04.030 --> 00:17:05.740
when I issue a read or a write?

00:17:05.740 --> 00:17:08.200
So typically what I like
to walk people through

00:17:08.200 --> 00:17:11.670
is the life of a read
and the life of a write.

00:17:11.670 --> 00:17:13.385
At kind of a high level--

00:17:13.385 --> 00:17:14.260
you can see it here--

00:17:14.260 --> 00:17:17.410
I think it's easier to start
with the life of a write.

00:17:17.410 --> 00:17:18.290
We have a client.

00:17:18.290 --> 00:17:20.079
These are typically
in a VM, although they

00:17:20.079 --> 00:17:21.819
could be other places.

00:17:21.819 --> 00:17:23.980
They go to our load balancers.

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:26.859
And from there, they go to a
front end, which sends them

00:17:26.859 --> 00:17:28.870
on to the very span server.

00:17:28.870 --> 00:17:32.560
A span server is what
actually stores---

00:17:32.560 --> 00:17:34.960
what would be a good
description of this--

00:17:34.960 --> 00:17:37.040
slices of your data.

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:39.760
So we refer to them
as Paxos groups.

00:17:39.760 --> 00:17:41.890
These are basically
data containers

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:45.670
that are spread across multiple
zones for availability.

00:17:45.670 --> 00:17:49.420
Commits have to go to two out
of three, or three out of five,

00:17:49.420 --> 00:17:52.360
or some number that
forms a quorum.

00:17:52.360 --> 00:17:54.930
As you can see it here,

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:57.730
The span server in Zone A will
take that write that it gets,

00:17:57.730 --> 00:18:00.760
and it will forward it
on to Zone B and Zone c.

00:18:00.760 --> 00:18:03.090
It will wait until two
of those come back,

00:18:03.090 --> 00:18:05.650
or two total come back,
both its local one and one

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:07.030
of the others.

00:18:07.030 --> 00:18:08.960
And then it'll return
back to the client,

00:18:08.960 --> 00:18:11.420
and that's the point at
which it becomes durable.

00:18:11.420 --> 00:18:13.120
You can see in reference
here, Colossus,

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:15.578
which I mentioned earlier, this
is our big distributed file

00:18:15.578 --> 00:18:17.360
system.

00:18:17.360 --> 00:18:21.820
Span servers, we
run tons of them.

00:18:21.820 --> 00:18:25.240
Internally, Spanner is
heavily, heavily used,

00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:27.610
so we're talking about
thousands to tens

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:30.550
of thousand of these
running in the data center.

00:18:30.550 --> 00:18:34.965
Your data resides on
a fraction of those.

00:18:34.965 --> 00:18:37.090
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Before
you move on to the reads,

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:39.972
really quickly just to
level set, because we live

00:18:39.972 --> 00:18:40.680
and breathe this.

00:18:40.680 --> 00:18:46.360
So Spanner is a synchronously
replicated database.

00:18:46.360 --> 00:18:49.720
What that means is that
we have full copies

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:55.210
of the data, database data, in
at least three, if not more,

00:18:55.210 --> 00:18:56.610
availability zones.

00:18:56.610 --> 00:18:58.310
The copies of data
are in Colossus,

00:18:58.310 --> 00:19:00.310
which is the distributed
file system that Andrew

00:19:00.310 --> 00:19:01.180
talked about.

00:19:01.180 --> 00:19:05.110
And because we have this
disaggregated storage

00:19:05.110 --> 00:19:08.560
and compute, span servers
here are the compute units

00:19:08.560 --> 00:19:10.180
that act on the data.

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:13.240
So the questions
around, well, what

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:14.650
happens if a server
goes down, do

00:19:14.650 --> 00:19:16.450
you have to reload
all the data in memory

00:19:16.450 --> 00:19:18.700
in a different server,
are kind of orthogonal,

00:19:18.700 --> 00:19:22.210
because the data is always
in the storage layer.

00:19:22.210 --> 00:19:24.130
And all these compute
units or span servers

00:19:24.130 --> 00:19:28.420
are doing is being responsible
for serving the data.

00:19:28.420 --> 00:19:30.280
And so when Andrew
talks about, a client

00:19:30.280 --> 00:19:32.620
sends a write,
what they're doing

00:19:32.620 --> 00:19:36.340
is, they're saying
write key with value x.

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:38.260
And that means that
there's an operation that

00:19:38.260 --> 00:19:40.600
needs to happen in any
one of the compute units.

00:19:40.600 --> 00:19:41.975
This is really
important, really.

00:19:41.975 --> 00:19:43.860
Any one of the compute
units can pick it up.

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:46.320
We use Paxos to figure
out who the leader is.

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:48.040
We don't have a
concept of master.

00:19:48.040 --> 00:19:50.260
And there is a
paper out on Paxos.

00:19:50.260 --> 00:19:52.630
Essentially, it's a
group consensus protocol

00:19:52.630 --> 00:19:55.390
that allows you to choose
at-most-once semantics,

00:19:55.390 --> 00:20:00.935
essentially, for a given point
in time or a given operation.

00:20:00.935 --> 00:20:02.707
Does that summarize it OK?

00:20:02.707 --> 00:20:04.540
But I just wanted to
make sure we understand

00:20:04.540 --> 00:20:05.310
what we're talking about.

00:20:05.310 --> 00:20:06.102
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah.

00:20:06.102 --> 00:20:08.800
I think a few other things
that are happening here

00:20:08.800 --> 00:20:12.862
is, during writes, we do use
TrueTime to pick a timestamp.

00:20:12.862 --> 00:20:14.320
Those timestamps
are used to create

00:20:14.320 --> 00:20:17.920
an ordering, which we talk about
as being externally consistent.

00:20:17.920 --> 00:20:22.990
That means that, if you do
two successive operations,

00:20:22.990 --> 00:20:25.510
they will get
increasing timestamps.

00:20:25.510 --> 00:20:28.450
So an operation that happens
after another operation

00:20:28.450 --> 00:20:33.740
by the concept of time will
actually happen that way.

00:20:33.740 --> 00:20:36.820
I think the other thing that's
interesting from this diagram

00:20:36.820 --> 00:20:38.770
is really to think
about, where does

00:20:38.770 --> 00:20:41.050
high availability come from?

00:20:41.050 --> 00:20:43.240
And high availability
really comes from the fact

00:20:43.240 --> 00:20:45.280
that we are replicating data.

00:20:45.280 --> 00:20:49.780
So for example, if Zone C
falls off the face of the map--

00:20:49.780 --> 00:20:51.940
earthquake, California,
that sort of thing--

00:20:51.940 --> 00:20:54.430
we can still continue
to issue rights

00:20:54.430 --> 00:20:58.600
to Zone a or Zone B. If we
have a crashing bug in one

00:20:58.600 --> 00:21:00.860
of the span servers,
for example,

00:21:00.860 --> 00:21:02.290
the other ones can pick it up.

00:21:02.290 --> 00:21:05.050
And so you get high
availability by taking

00:21:05.050 --> 00:21:07.652
these components
of blast radiuses

00:21:07.652 --> 00:21:09.610
and what happens when
they fail and making sure

00:21:09.610 --> 00:21:12.100
that you have other
things to capture them up.

00:21:12.100 --> 00:21:13.100
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yeah.

00:21:13.100 --> 00:21:14.642
Do you want to walk
through the read?

00:21:14.642 --> 00:21:15.520
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah.

00:21:15.520 --> 00:21:18.100
A read is also very simple.

00:21:18.100 --> 00:21:20.830
I drew it here showing that
the reads don't necessarily

00:21:20.830 --> 00:21:23.470
have to go to the
leaders in order

00:21:23.470 --> 00:21:25.360
to be strongly consistent.

00:21:25.360 --> 00:21:27.310
So a client might do a read.

00:21:27.310 --> 00:21:30.500
He might go to one of the other
replicas of the [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:30.500 --> 00:21:32.360
group.

00:21:32.360 --> 00:21:35.500
Now, when he gets
there, he basically

00:21:35.500 --> 00:21:39.100
picks a timestamp, which is what
we call now plus epsilon, which

00:21:39.100 --> 00:21:43.790
is typically a time at which
we know no writes that we could

00:21:43.790 --> 00:21:46.750
have seen were committed.

00:21:46.750 --> 00:21:50.110
That front end will
basically say, OK, pass it

00:21:50.110 --> 00:21:51.010
on to a span server.

00:21:51.010 --> 00:21:54.400
The span server will say,
OK, do I have all of the data

00:21:54.400 --> 00:21:55.660
up to that time?

00:21:55.660 --> 00:21:58.990
And if enough time has passed--
because we're talking order

00:21:58.990 --> 00:22:03.190
milliseconds, and
writes may be going on--

00:22:03.190 --> 00:22:05.603
he will actually just
return the data then.

00:22:05.603 --> 00:22:08.020
If he doesn't have the data,
he'll reach out to the leader

00:22:08.020 --> 00:22:12.100
and say, dude, I hear you
have some data for me.

00:22:12.100 --> 00:22:15.490
And the span servers will
say, yes, here you go.

00:22:15.490 --> 00:22:19.570
Either here's the data
you need, or you actually

00:22:19.570 --> 00:22:21.400
have all the data, I
just haven't told you

00:22:21.400 --> 00:22:22.750
that you have all the data.

00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:25.320
And in that case, the read
will be performed locally.

00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:26.320
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yeah.

00:22:26.320 --> 00:22:29.530
So I think the point here that
is different from other systems

00:22:29.530 --> 00:22:33.850
is that we are, in
almost all cases,

00:22:33.850 --> 00:22:37.952
serving the data from
the local replica.

00:22:37.952 --> 00:22:39.910
There are other system
that ship buffers around

00:22:39.910 --> 00:22:40.810
when they're replicated.

00:22:40.810 --> 00:22:42.300
We're not shipping
buffers around.

00:22:42.300 --> 00:22:43.960
What we're saying
is, hey, what's

00:22:43.960 --> 00:22:46.450
the latest timestamp
that I can serve data at?

00:22:46.450 --> 00:22:47.600
Do I have it or not?

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:50.530
And if the answer is yes, you
have it, then you go serve it,

00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:53.600
or you wait for the data to be
shipped as part of the Paxos

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:55.480
protocol separately.

00:22:55.480 --> 00:22:57.970
So you're not actually shipping
megabytes and megabytes

00:22:57.970 --> 00:22:58.720
of buffers around.

00:22:58.720 --> 00:22:59.512
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah.

00:22:59.512 --> 00:23:02.050
And just to give you an
order on the timing here,

00:23:02.050 --> 00:23:04.720
you could imagine that zones
A, B, and C are actually

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:06.340
all within a region.

00:23:06.340 --> 00:23:08.380
In that case, your
writes are on the order

00:23:08.380 --> 00:23:11.640
of five to 10 milliseconds,
because that's

00:23:11.640 --> 00:23:15.500
what it takes to create a quorum
across those data centers.

00:23:15.500 --> 00:23:17.380
For a read, it would
be much, much faster,

00:23:17.380 --> 00:23:19.828
because you're only reaching
out to one data center.

00:23:19.828 --> 00:23:22.120
So once we get through the
load balancer and everything

00:23:22.120 --> 00:23:25.000
like that, I think it's
in the three to five

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:26.580
range from external cloud.

00:23:26.580 --> 00:23:28.350
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yeah.

00:23:28.350 --> 00:23:34.090
All right, so let's talk
about some of the aha moments

00:23:34.090 --> 00:23:35.010
where we--

00:23:35.010 --> 00:23:38.590
well, you at least-- chose to
be a distributed systems guy--

00:23:38.590 --> 00:23:41.840
sorry, to a database person from
a distributed systems person.

00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:44.620
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah, so I
think people say, well,

00:23:44.620 --> 00:23:47.020
what's great about Spanner,
what should I take away?

00:23:47.020 --> 00:23:50.140
I think there are two things
that I typically reference.

00:23:50.140 --> 00:23:53.560
One is that, as a
distributed systems engineer,

00:23:53.560 --> 00:23:56.200
you're growing up, and everybody
tells you you cannot trust

00:23:56.200 --> 00:23:56.750
time.

00:23:56.750 --> 00:23:59.470
The clock on your server
is absolutely something

00:23:59.470 --> 00:24:00.610
you can't trust right.

00:24:00.610 --> 00:24:05.830
And you develop sort of this
innate fear of that thing.

00:24:05.830 --> 00:24:08.170
It's like legendary.

00:24:08.170 --> 00:24:11.680
And you also probably experience
some really bad things when you

00:24:11.680 --> 00:24:14.650
decide to trust time anyways.

00:24:14.650 --> 00:24:17.200
Having the ability to
actually trust time,

00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:20.080
and we are now 10
years later, and I

00:24:20.080 --> 00:24:24.550
can tell you that I completely
trust our time system.

00:24:24.550 --> 00:24:27.820
It has made my life
so much easier.

00:24:27.820 --> 00:24:35.193
Being able to basically have
a source of global ordering

00:24:35.193 --> 00:24:37.360
that I don't actually have
to reach out and talk to,

00:24:37.360 --> 00:24:41.470
that I can reference locally,
is a huge, huge, huge just

00:24:41.470 --> 00:24:43.260
thinking shift.

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:46.780
And it took us actually quite
a long time to get over that.

00:24:46.780 --> 00:24:49.810
When I said we did two or
three versions of this system,

00:24:49.810 --> 00:24:51.430
some of the earlier
ones actually

00:24:51.430 --> 00:24:55.540
worked more on logical clock
passing or figuring out how

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:57.390
to weave these things through.

00:24:57.390 --> 00:24:59.140
And weaving timestamps
through your system

00:24:59.140 --> 00:25:01.150
actually has some really
bad effects, usually,

00:25:01.150 --> 00:25:02.230
on your client.

00:25:02.230 --> 00:25:05.710
You're like, OK, well, this
person can give me a timestamp.

00:25:05.710 --> 00:25:08.230
Now I can take this timestamp
and give it to someone else.

00:25:08.230 --> 00:25:11.090
That means that the application
actually has to see it.

00:25:11.090 --> 00:25:13.450
And so being able to trust
time and use that, in a way,

00:25:13.450 --> 00:25:18.350
actually has really good upper
level API effects as well.

00:25:18.350 --> 00:25:21.310
The second sort of aha moment
was that we spent a lot of time

00:25:21.310 --> 00:25:25.120
not implementing
transactions in Bigtable,

00:25:25.120 --> 00:25:26.990
for a variety of reasons.

00:25:26.990 --> 00:25:30.303
And the main reason is
that the availability

00:25:30.303 --> 00:25:31.720
of any one of our
Bigtable tablets

00:25:31.720 --> 00:25:34.870
was not sufficient enough
to support transactions.

00:25:34.870 --> 00:25:38.290
And so what Paxos and
groups bring us is they

00:25:38.290 --> 00:25:41.260
bring us highly
available participants.

00:25:41.260 --> 00:25:43.570
So the other aha
moment in here is

00:25:43.570 --> 00:25:46.217
you can do a lot with highly
available participants.

00:25:46.217 --> 00:25:47.800
You can trust them
to be coordinators.

00:25:47.800 --> 00:25:49.210
You can trust them
to hold locks.

00:25:49.210 --> 00:25:51.170
You can trust them to
do things like that.

00:25:51.170 --> 00:25:54.040
So it's really the combination
of both trusting time

00:25:54.040 --> 00:25:56.980
and having highly
available components

00:25:56.980 --> 00:25:59.775
upon which to build transactions
that may expand are unique.

00:25:59.775 --> 00:26:01.900
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yeah,
I remember get time of day

00:26:01.900 --> 00:26:04.960
was an evil thing and you
never put it in your code.

00:26:04.960 --> 00:26:08.590
But TrueTime along
with Paxos gives us

00:26:08.590 --> 00:26:11.140
that external consistency of--

00:26:11.140 --> 00:26:12.640
and I love saying
this to customers,

00:26:12.640 --> 00:26:14.390
because they're like,
I don't believe you.

00:26:14.390 --> 00:26:16.600
But you can write from
anywhere in the world

00:26:16.600 --> 00:26:20.220
into a Spanner database, and
you can literally say, I wrote--

00:26:20.220 --> 00:26:23.170
like I incremented my
bank account with $50--

00:26:23.170 --> 00:26:24.910
and anywhere in
the world, somebody

00:26:24.910 --> 00:26:27.960
can read that timestamp and
actually get an answer for,

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:30.198
it was plus 50, which
is an amazing thing to

00:26:30.198 --> 00:26:30.990
ANDREW FIKES: Have.

00:26:30.990 --> 00:26:32.890
Yeah, $50?

00:26:32.890 --> 00:26:35.350
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yes,
it's better than 0.

00:26:35.350 --> 00:26:39.070
OK, so let's move on
to a little bit more

00:26:39.070 --> 00:26:42.520
on the nostalgia piece,
which I find exciting.

00:26:42.520 --> 00:26:46.410
But we talked about
scaling systems,

00:26:46.410 --> 00:26:48.760
but you also have
to scale teams.

00:26:48.760 --> 00:26:52.030
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah, I
think every good project--

00:26:52.030 --> 00:26:54.260
and I talked to
some people earlier

00:26:54.260 --> 00:26:56.520
with sort of small teams.

00:26:56.520 --> 00:26:58.720
Spanner, of course,
started with a small team.

00:26:58.720 --> 00:27:01.330
It started with four
people in an office.

00:27:01.330 --> 00:27:04.000
I think this picture is in 2012.

00:27:04.000 --> 00:27:06.230
It shows about
five or six people.

00:27:06.230 --> 00:27:10.720
We look heavily engaged in
a particular event probably,

00:27:10.720 --> 00:27:15.280
in terms of a server going down
or us debugging sort of things.

00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:17.287
It's kind of fun to look back.

00:27:17.287 --> 00:27:18.620
Chris is down here in the front.

00:27:18.620 --> 00:27:21.205
He looks much younger
in that photo.

00:27:21.205 --> 00:27:23.330
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: There
were less critical systems

00:27:23.330 --> 00:27:25.480
running on Spanner at the time.

00:27:25.480 --> 00:27:27.370
And I also want you to
talk about this one.

00:27:27.370 --> 00:27:28.205
It's super awesome.

00:27:28.205 --> 00:27:31.180
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah, so
this was my cube mate.

00:27:31.180 --> 00:27:33.520
This is Mike.

00:27:33.520 --> 00:27:35.170
He's a fan of Ascii art.

00:27:35.170 --> 00:27:37.250
These were our first 100 users.

00:27:37.250 --> 00:27:40.840
So every now and then
you see a $20 bill

00:27:40.840 --> 00:27:44.220
posted on the front of a
deli or something like that.

00:27:44.220 --> 00:27:46.270
This was our screen
as we watched

00:27:46.270 --> 00:27:48.160
internal users adopt Spanner.

00:27:48.160 --> 00:27:53.890
And when we hit 100 on
our lovely VT320 here,

00:27:53.890 --> 00:27:54.820
we had a little party.

00:27:54.820 --> 00:27:57.028
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: I was
actually there for this one.

00:27:57.028 --> 00:28:00.327
So I want to have people
look at this, and then this.

00:28:00.327 --> 00:28:02.410
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah, so a
couple years later, we're

00:28:02.410 --> 00:28:03.710
getting a little bigger.

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:07.810
We added a few fish into the
mix, as any good team has.

00:28:07.810 --> 00:28:10.528
And a few of them I
think are still kicking.

00:28:10.528 --> 00:28:12.820
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Andrew
and I commissioned this logo,

00:28:12.820 --> 00:28:14.403
because, of course,
we were a service,

00:28:14.403 --> 00:28:15.610
and we needed a product.

00:28:15.610 --> 00:28:17.750
And we needed a logo,
so we had this logo.

00:28:17.750 --> 00:28:19.570
And if you don't
understand what that is,

00:28:19.570 --> 00:28:23.140
it's obvious this is a
Spanner turning Google.

00:28:23.140 --> 00:28:25.800
ANDREW FIKES: It's a Spanner.

00:28:25.800 --> 00:28:27.065
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: And then--

00:28:27.065 --> 00:28:28.690
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah,
so this is actually

00:28:28.690 --> 00:28:30.740
a couple of years
ago at this point.

00:28:30.740 --> 00:28:32.690
I think the key thing
to take away from this

00:28:32.690 --> 00:28:35.410
is that building a
distributed system that

00:28:35.410 --> 00:28:37.420
is as highly available
and actually has

00:28:37.420 --> 00:28:40.330
all of the features that
you all know and love

00:28:40.330 --> 00:28:43.323
is actually a relatively
complex problem.

00:28:43.323 --> 00:28:45.490
The other thing that I've
really grown to appreciate

00:28:45.490 --> 00:28:48.880
is that database problems
and systems problems

00:28:48.880 --> 00:28:50.920
are actually full
stack problems.

00:28:50.920 --> 00:28:55.930
And so the amount of complexity
that is in these systems now

00:28:55.930 --> 00:28:58.000
is substantially more.

00:28:58.000 --> 00:29:00.490
When we built GFS in
the early days, which

00:29:00.490 --> 00:29:02.590
was our first
distributed file system,

00:29:02.590 --> 00:29:04.810
we almost decided not to do it.

00:29:04.810 --> 00:29:08.110
Because we thought it was
too complex and nothing

00:29:08.110 --> 00:29:09.830
that complex would ever work.

00:29:09.830 --> 00:29:12.580
It turns out it's the simplest
thing we've ever built,

00:29:12.580 --> 00:29:16.725
and Spanner has many more
ages of complexity on it.

00:29:16.725 --> 00:29:18.850
I think the other thing
you can see in this picture

00:29:18.850 --> 00:29:22.720
is this enormous giant
wrench in the background.

00:29:22.720 --> 00:29:24.520
That actually is
used for trains.

00:29:24.520 --> 00:29:27.250
So if you ever need to
change a train wheel,

00:29:27.250 --> 00:29:29.537
we have a wrench for you.

00:29:29.537 --> 00:29:31.870
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yeah, we
actually have three of them,

00:29:31.870 --> 00:29:32.680
because we don't--

00:29:32.680 --> 00:29:33.580
ANDREW FIKES: We have three?

00:29:33.580 --> 00:29:35.497
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yes,
we have three wrenches

00:29:35.497 --> 00:29:36.980
on three different sites.

00:29:36.980 --> 00:29:40.565
And I think the takeaway--
because we are highly available

00:29:40.565 --> 00:29:42.580
and replicated.

00:29:42.580 --> 00:29:46.420
So the thing for me
was-- because for me this

00:29:46.420 --> 00:29:47.470
has also been a journey.

00:29:47.470 --> 00:29:52.270
And building teams to have
that sort of rigor and mindset

00:29:52.270 --> 00:29:54.730
when we're doing infrastructure
that we have to have,

00:29:54.730 --> 00:29:56.680
like we make no
compromises, we don't

00:29:56.680 --> 00:30:01.330
cut corners, and the processes
for around that stuff is--

00:30:01.330 --> 00:30:02.363
like it changes, right?

00:30:02.363 --> 00:30:05.030
The process that works with five
people, which is nudging people

00:30:05.030 --> 00:30:07.883
to say, hey, fix this bug,
versus 50 people versus more

00:30:07.883 --> 00:30:09.050
than 50 people is different.

00:30:09.050 --> 00:30:12.460
And so it's really been
an exciting journey.

00:30:12.460 --> 00:30:15.610
And Andrew is--
and I am as well--

00:30:15.610 --> 00:30:19.958
very excited about the culture
and the team cohesion and all

00:30:19.958 --> 00:30:20.500
those things.

00:30:20.500 --> 00:30:21.670
And they're real things.

00:30:21.670 --> 00:30:25.240
You really have to worry about
culture and respect and all

00:30:25.240 --> 00:30:27.190
that stuff as you grow people.

00:30:27.190 --> 00:30:29.470
And of course, in case
that wasn't obvious,

00:30:29.470 --> 00:30:33.280
we actually have Spanner people
distributed in multiple areas,

00:30:33.280 --> 00:30:34.210
too.

00:30:34.210 --> 00:30:36.310
ANDREW FIKES: We have
people in New York,

00:30:36.310 --> 00:30:37.510
the Seattle-Kirkland area.

00:30:37.510 --> 00:30:39.490
A good portion of
our operations staff

00:30:39.490 --> 00:30:42.670
is actually in Sydney,
where I think they're just

00:30:42.670 --> 00:30:43.750
starting to wake up.

00:30:43.750 --> 00:30:44.020
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA:
In Boston now.

00:30:44.020 --> 00:30:45.280
ANDREW FIKES: And Boston.

00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:47.620
And managing a
large team, finding

00:30:47.620 --> 00:30:50.250
good chunks of work for them
is definitely a challenge.

00:30:50.250 --> 00:30:51.250
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yeah.

00:30:51.250 --> 00:30:56.140
OK, so let's go into the more
fun parts, which is, let's

00:30:56.140 --> 00:30:58.180
opinionate.

00:30:58.180 --> 00:31:02.295
So let's touch on
a few things that I

00:31:02.295 --> 00:31:03.670
know customers
have talked to me,

00:31:03.670 --> 00:31:06.020
and I know that you're
also passionate about.

00:31:06.020 --> 00:31:09.190
So first of them is CAP theorem.

00:31:09.190 --> 00:31:11.495
So does Spanner break
the CAP theorem, Andrew?

00:31:11.495 --> 00:31:13.120
ANDREW FIKES: This
is a great question.

00:31:15.760 --> 00:31:18.850
For years, I hated
the CAP theorem.

00:31:18.850 --> 00:31:21.500
Then I started sharing an
office with Eric Brewer,

00:31:21.500 --> 00:31:24.160
and he turns out to be
an absolutely nice guy,

00:31:24.160 --> 00:31:25.720
so it's great.

00:31:25.720 --> 00:31:27.815
And I think, as I talk
it over with Eric--

00:31:27.815 --> 00:31:30.190
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Eric is
the author of the CAP theorem,

00:31:30.190 --> 00:31:30.690
by the way.

00:31:30.690 --> 00:31:32.140
Also wrote a paper
on how Spanner

00:31:32.140 --> 00:31:33.775
doesn't break the CAP theorem.

00:31:33.775 --> 00:31:35.900
ANDREW FIKES: As you talk
things through with Eric,

00:31:35.900 --> 00:31:38.350
his real point here is
the CAP theorem was really

00:31:38.350 --> 00:31:40.660
designed to help you think.

00:31:40.660 --> 00:31:42.430
And it's really also
designed for you

00:31:42.430 --> 00:31:45.490
to think about the extremes.

00:31:45.490 --> 00:31:49.000
So it's really about
100% situations.

00:31:49.000 --> 00:31:53.020
And in real life,
nothing is 100%.

00:31:53.020 --> 00:31:54.660
And so when you
really look-- earlier,

00:31:54.660 --> 00:31:57.160
when we talked about things
like the building blocks that we

00:31:57.160 --> 00:31:59.950
have, our network, file
systems, and various other sorts

00:31:59.950 --> 00:32:01.060
of things--

00:32:01.060 --> 00:32:03.310
we're able to take things
like partitions, which

00:32:03.310 --> 00:32:06.550
might be really common in
most people's networks or most

00:32:06.550 --> 00:32:08.860
people's WANs, and
say, hey, can we

00:32:08.860 --> 00:32:10.960
think about these
things different?

00:32:10.960 --> 00:32:14.050
Can we actually look at
the data and understand

00:32:14.050 --> 00:32:17.560
how often partitions
actually impact things?

00:32:17.560 --> 00:32:19.750
When a partition
happens, for example,

00:32:19.750 --> 00:32:23.350
does it also impact
the end application?

00:32:23.350 --> 00:32:26.170
Would it actually
impact a quorum?

00:32:26.170 --> 00:32:28.180
And so as you start
to look at these

00:32:28.180 --> 00:32:31.750
both from a mathematical
perspective, the probabilities,

00:32:31.750 --> 00:32:36.150
but you also look on the
ground data, what you find

00:32:36.150 --> 00:32:39.140
is that partitions
are actually much,

00:32:39.140 --> 00:32:41.680
much less likely than the
other sources of error

00:32:41.680 --> 00:32:42.677
in your system.

00:32:42.677 --> 00:32:44.260
And this is one of
the arguments we've

00:32:44.260 --> 00:32:47.170
made about TrueTime, whether
it be more accurate than CPU

00:32:47.170 --> 00:32:49.270
failures, we can
sort of make it here.

00:32:49.270 --> 00:32:52.270
It turns out that most sources
of unavailability in our system

00:32:52.270 --> 00:32:55.005
have things to do
with like users.

00:32:55.005 --> 00:32:56.380
I don't know how
many map reduces

00:32:56.380 --> 00:32:59.440
have been run in the world that
have taken down a system they

00:32:59.440 --> 00:33:01.350
weren't supposed to.

00:33:01.350 --> 00:33:07.210
They have things to do with
RSRE teams, missing a comma,

00:33:07.210 --> 00:33:10.150
turning it into a period,
something like that.

00:33:10.150 --> 00:33:12.730
So it's those sources-- and
operator error is actually

00:33:12.730 --> 00:33:15.980
much, much lower
than user error--

00:33:15.980 --> 00:33:18.470
those sorts of events happen
much more than partitions.

00:33:18.470 --> 00:33:20.750
And so once you
basically say, OK,

00:33:20.750 --> 00:33:22.300
let's assume partitions
are not going

00:33:22.300 --> 00:33:24.280
to be the source of
the unavailability,

00:33:24.280 --> 00:33:26.500
can you build a
system that actually

00:33:26.500 --> 00:33:31.250
has really good availability
and gives you consistency?

00:33:31.250 --> 00:33:33.690
And so that's sort
of where we land.

00:33:33.690 --> 00:33:36.980
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA:
Yeah, so I think

00:33:36.980 --> 00:33:42.954
we trade off partition tolerance
for availability mostly.

00:33:42.954 --> 00:33:43.672
Cool.

00:33:43.672 --> 00:33:45.130
So the other
interesting thing here

00:33:45.130 --> 00:33:50.170
is, Spanner evolved from a
NoSQL to a Bigtable world

00:33:50.170 --> 00:33:52.640
into a fully SQL database.

00:33:52.640 --> 00:33:57.793
And so how has your thoughts
on NoSQL to 3SQL evolved?

00:33:57.793 --> 00:33:59.460
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah,
so many years ago, I

00:33:59.460 --> 00:34:02.860
was invited to give
a talk about NoSQL.

00:34:02.860 --> 00:34:06.490
I knew nothing about
NoSQL, so I went and read

00:34:06.490 --> 00:34:08.560
Wikipedia like a good person.

00:34:08.560 --> 00:34:11.500
And it said, a series
of distributed systems

00:34:11.500 --> 00:34:13.610
as created from the
lineage of Bigtable.

00:34:13.610 --> 00:34:16.300
And I said, oh, OK,
I know Bigtable.

00:34:16.300 --> 00:34:19.120
Maybe I do know
something about NoSQL.

00:34:19.120 --> 00:34:21.130
Typically when people
talk about NoSQL,

00:34:21.130 --> 00:34:25.179
they're talking about a
horizontally scalable key value

00:34:25.179 --> 00:34:27.219
system.

00:34:27.219 --> 00:34:30.219
Typically, they also talk
about some properties

00:34:30.219 --> 00:34:32.380
around eventual consistency.

00:34:32.380 --> 00:34:35.949
I was, for years, a huge
fan of eventual consistency.

00:34:35.949 --> 00:34:38.110
I built Bigtable's first
replication system.

00:34:38.110 --> 00:34:39.699
It was eventually consistent.

00:34:39.699 --> 00:34:41.860
I was totally
convinced that anybody

00:34:41.860 --> 00:34:45.159
who wouldn't want an eventually
consistent system was wrong.

00:34:45.159 --> 00:34:48.628
I'm here to tell you I was
wrong on leaps and bounds.

00:34:48.628 --> 00:34:50.170
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA:
We got it on tape.

00:34:50.170 --> 00:34:51.370
ANDREW FIKES: Yes.

00:34:51.370 --> 00:34:54.370
What you sort of learn over time
with an eventually consistent

00:34:54.370 --> 00:34:58.030
system is you actually go
to start to work with users.

00:34:58.030 --> 00:35:00.220
And you see users try
to build applications

00:35:00.220 --> 00:35:03.290
on top of an eventually
consistent system.

00:35:03.290 --> 00:35:04.570
Yes, it's kind of fun.

00:35:04.570 --> 00:35:06.070
We get to use that
part of our brain

00:35:06.070 --> 00:35:08.290
that creates a complicated
algorithm that only works

00:35:08.290 --> 00:35:10.390
under these certain
situations, and maybe the user

00:35:10.390 --> 00:35:13.263
sees it this way, or maybe the
user doesn't see it that way.

00:35:13.263 --> 00:35:15.430
But at the end of day, it's
just not really any fun.

00:35:15.430 --> 00:35:17.500
And our goal is
to build products.

00:35:17.500 --> 00:35:20.650
Our goal is not to build
complicated algorithms that

00:35:20.650 --> 00:35:22.330
fix little edge cases.

00:35:22.330 --> 00:35:24.680
We want to get a product
in front of our customers.

00:35:24.680 --> 00:35:27.910
We want to see that product
quickly and get feedback on it.

00:35:27.910 --> 00:35:31.690
And consistent systems
give you that property.

00:35:31.690 --> 00:35:35.800
I sort of had the same
idea around transactions.

00:35:35.800 --> 00:35:37.360
The very first
versions of Bigtable,

00:35:37.360 --> 00:35:39.640
for example, have
a complicated split

00:35:39.640 --> 00:35:42.040
and merge protocol in them.

00:35:42.040 --> 00:35:43.670
We kind of knew
what we were doing.

00:35:43.670 --> 00:35:45.760
We hand-rolled our
own transactions.

00:35:45.760 --> 00:35:49.390
I think we did it six times,
because we got it wrong time

00:35:49.390 --> 00:35:51.130
after time after time.

00:35:51.130 --> 00:35:52.960
Having primitives
around consistency

00:35:52.960 --> 00:35:54.760
in transactions
as building blocks

00:35:54.760 --> 00:35:56.620
are incredibly productive.

00:35:56.620 --> 00:36:02.810
They let you sort of leverage
those things onto other things.

00:36:02.810 --> 00:36:07.030
I think the other
thing that you find

00:36:07.030 --> 00:36:10.570
in this kind of NoSQL
versus NewSQL debate

00:36:10.570 --> 00:36:13.420
is really this idea of SQL.

00:36:13.420 --> 00:36:14.830
SQL's great.

00:36:14.830 --> 00:36:17.560
I think it's a
perfectly fine language.

00:36:17.560 --> 00:36:18.700
Tuples are great.

00:36:18.700 --> 00:36:21.380
I think types are
incredibly useful.

00:36:21.380 --> 00:36:23.410
We've seen systems on
the key value space

00:36:23.410 --> 00:36:25.330
adopt types more and more.

00:36:25.330 --> 00:36:27.040
As we start to
think about moving

00:36:27.040 --> 00:36:29.830
compute closer to the
data, which is a trend,

00:36:29.830 --> 00:36:32.500
then things databases have
been doing closer to years,

00:36:32.500 --> 00:36:34.360
you really need a
language to express that.

00:36:34.360 --> 00:36:36.670
You need to understand types
so you can drive it down

00:36:36.670 --> 00:36:38.942
to the processor level.

00:36:38.942 --> 00:36:40.150
And so SQLs a great language.

00:36:40.150 --> 00:36:42.880
I think where SQL tends to
get a bad rap is it's often

00:36:42.880 --> 00:36:46.090
associated with vertically
scaled systems, things

00:36:46.090 --> 00:36:47.890
that only fit in a box.

00:36:47.890 --> 00:36:52.870
Or they have challenges
around multi-tenancy.

00:36:52.870 --> 00:36:54.820
I think Spanner does
a much better job

00:36:54.820 --> 00:36:58.060
on the horizontal
scaling aspects,

00:36:58.060 --> 00:36:59.770
so that gives us some power.

00:36:59.770 --> 00:37:02.350
We've also always run in a
multi-tenant environment.

00:37:02.350 --> 00:37:06.310
Google has tens and twenties
and hundreds of thousands

00:37:06.310 --> 00:37:08.080
of databases internally,
all competing

00:37:08.080 --> 00:37:10.060
for the same resources,
so multi-tenancy

00:37:10.060 --> 00:37:12.790
has been a core component
of what we do for years.

00:37:12.790 --> 00:37:15.730
And so those sorts of
properties of SQL systems,

00:37:15.730 --> 00:37:18.925
we can look at them
slightly differently.

00:37:18.925 --> 00:37:20.050
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Totally.

00:37:20.050 --> 00:37:23.650
So we talk about consistency,
we talked about SQL, NoSQL.

00:37:23.650 --> 00:37:25.900
How do you think it
kind of comes together

00:37:25.900 --> 00:37:29.758
to power Google's
cloud offerings?

00:37:29.758 --> 00:37:30.550
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah.

00:37:36.235 --> 00:37:37.930
Spanner is a workhorse.

00:37:37.930 --> 00:37:41.770
We use it in many, many
different ways at Google.

00:37:41.770 --> 00:37:44.470
We use it both for
things of scale, so

00:37:44.470 --> 00:37:46.870
things that are
petabytes that look

00:37:46.870 --> 00:37:50.890
kind of like traditional
indexing and batch workloads.

00:37:50.890 --> 00:37:54.550
We use it for very critical
high availability loads.

00:37:54.550 --> 00:37:56.200
I was just at a talk
right before this

00:37:56.200 --> 00:37:58.450
where it was
performance benchmarking

00:37:58.450 --> 00:38:01.150
around VM boot-up
times, and was talking

00:38:01.150 --> 00:38:03.790
about the complexity of
programming the control plane.

00:38:03.790 --> 00:38:05.860
That control plane
is done in Spanner.

00:38:05.860 --> 00:38:08.230
So our ability to
spin things up quickly

00:38:08.230 --> 00:38:11.530
and have them highly available
is dependent on Spanner.

00:38:11.530 --> 00:38:18.490
We see it in sort of the
SaaS application space, where

00:38:18.490 --> 00:38:20.470
you might take something
like Gmail where

00:38:20.470 --> 00:38:22.660
it takes a whole bunch
of users, maps them

00:38:22.660 --> 00:38:26.540
into a single database, and
gives them power that way.

00:38:26.540 --> 00:38:29.830
And we also see it in very
traditional database workloads.

00:38:29.830 --> 00:38:32.540
We use it for some of our
capacity planning for example,

00:38:32.540 --> 00:38:34.395
our capacity delivery systems.

00:38:34.395 --> 00:38:35.770
And so we really
have a workhorse

00:38:35.770 --> 00:38:37.603
of a system that really
goes from everything

00:38:37.603 --> 00:38:39.537
from the very big to
the very small work

00:38:39.537 --> 00:38:41.030
throughout our system.

00:38:41.030 --> 00:38:44.140
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: We actually
have a lot of, a lot, a lot,

00:38:44.140 --> 00:38:46.750
a lot of small use
cases that are all

00:38:46.750 --> 00:38:49.420
using Spanner for its
manageability and scale

00:38:49.420 --> 00:38:51.373
insurance and all that stuff.

00:38:51.373 --> 00:38:53.290
ANDREW FIKES: It's also
probably a good time--

00:38:53.290 --> 00:38:55.190
I know Deepti
mentioned it earlier--

00:38:55.190 --> 00:38:59.480
which is tomorrow my colleague
Dennis from the GCS team,

00:38:59.480 --> 00:39:01.600
which is our Google
Cloud Storage,

00:39:01.600 --> 00:39:05.560
is going to present how GCS
uses Spanner for its metadata.

00:39:05.560 --> 00:39:07.180
So our really
large scale systems

00:39:07.180 --> 00:39:09.690
are actually built with
Spanner as its backbone.

00:39:09.690 --> 00:39:12.190
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yeah that's
going to be Spanner Internals

00:39:12.190 --> 00:39:15.200
Part 2 tomorrow morning.

00:39:15.200 --> 00:39:16.750
So come watch us.

00:39:16.750 --> 00:39:21.790
But yeah, it's cool to see both
the GCSs and the Gmails as well

00:39:21.790 --> 00:39:24.490
as the Drives and a bunch of
other small internal systems,

00:39:24.490 --> 00:39:28.930
like supply chain
management, use Spanner.

00:39:28.930 --> 00:39:31.150
All right, so these are
some of the headlines

00:39:31.150 --> 00:39:33.370
for when we launched Spanner.

00:39:33.370 --> 00:39:36.190
There are more controversial
ones that I didn't put here.

00:39:36.190 --> 00:39:40.840
But I know both you and I have
thoughts on why we launch it

00:39:40.840 --> 00:39:42.915
externally, so why don't you--

00:39:42.915 --> 00:39:45.040
ANDREW FIKES: Yeah, I think
one of the great things

00:39:45.040 --> 00:39:48.670
about why I'm excited about
Cloud, its new workloads.

00:39:48.670 --> 00:39:51.710
I mentioned why I get
excited about infrastructure.

00:39:51.710 --> 00:39:53.210
It's because there's
new challenges,

00:39:53.210 --> 00:39:55.330
new workloads, new customers.

00:39:55.330 --> 00:39:58.605
I was very excited to
see examples of workloads

00:39:58.605 --> 00:39:59.980
that I saw internally
externally.

00:39:59.980 --> 00:40:02.530
I think Spanner's a great
building block for a lot

00:40:02.530 --> 00:40:05.490
of applications, being able
to get that out to you all ,

00:40:05.490 --> 00:40:09.740
and have you work it into your
systems was a big win for me.

00:40:09.740 --> 00:40:12.675
DEEPTI SRIVASTAVA: Yeah, I think
it was a while before we could

00:40:12.675 --> 00:40:14.050
convince ourselves
that we wanted

00:40:14.050 --> 00:40:16.780
to be a public product,
primarily because we didn't

00:40:16.780 --> 00:40:20.650
think that externally people had
the same challenges that we had

00:40:20.650 --> 00:40:21.710
internally.

00:40:21.710 --> 00:40:25.308
And I remember doing a
whole initial program

00:40:25.308 --> 00:40:27.100
of going out there and
talking to customers

00:40:27.100 --> 00:40:30.460
to see whether this would
be a useful system for them.

00:40:30.460 --> 00:40:33.860
Because at the time-- this
was like three years ago,

00:40:33.860 --> 00:40:36.910
which is dog years
in Cloud world--

00:40:36.910 --> 00:40:39.040
when we trying to
launch Spanner,

00:40:39.040 --> 00:40:41.770
we were cognizant
that people don't

00:40:41.770 --> 00:40:45.130
want just another cool tech
because Google built it.

00:40:45.130 --> 00:40:46.840
Especially on the
databases side,

00:40:46.840 --> 00:40:48.430
people want something that
solves their problems,

00:40:48.430 --> 00:40:49.870
and is this something
that is going

00:40:49.870 --> 00:40:51.120
to solve their problem or not?

00:40:51.120 --> 00:40:54.520
So we actually wanted to
be cognizant of user issues

00:40:54.520 --> 00:40:58.040
and see if this could solve our
user and customer pain points.

00:40:58.040 --> 00:41:00.880
And after serving
customers, we found

00:41:00.880 --> 00:41:04.085
that customers were coming
to this with a new internet

00:41:04.085 --> 00:41:05.710
age of everything
connected, everything

00:41:05.710 --> 00:41:07.780
always on, everything online.

00:41:07.780 --> 00:41:10.360
Customers wanted that
kind of scale insurance

00:41:10.360 --> 00:41:13.420
with horizontal scalability
and strong consistency

00:41:13.420 --> 00:41:14.530
and relational semantics.

00:41:14.530 --> 00:41:19.930
And so it was like a really good
time to be in the public eye.

00:41:19.930 --> 00:41:22.160
So thank you so much
for your time again.

